                        <origDate when-custom="1050/1075" notBefore="1050" notAfter="1075"
                           calendar="#gregorian">s. xi 3/4</origDate>
                     </origin>
                     <provenance type="#s" source="2709"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_1">Exeter,
                           Devon. Cathedral church of St Peter</orgName><p>Exeter script; see E. M.
                           Drage, Bishop Leofric and Exeter Cathedral Chapter (1050–1072), Oxford
                           DPhil thesis, 1978. Perhaps the "i liber Leofrici primi Exonensis [sic]
                           Episcopi ..." in 1506 Cat. (Oliver, <title>Lives</title>, p.
                        351).</p></provenance>
                     <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_456">Ramsey,
                           Huntingdonshire. Benedictine Abbey of Saint Mary the Virgin and St
                           Benedict</orgName><p>The Ramsey attribution was accepted (albeit with a
                           query) in both the first and second editions of Medieval Libraries of
                           Great Britain (1941, 1964). However, Ramsey was later rejected in favour
                           of Exeter in the Supplement (1987). In MLGB3 (2010-2015), the Ramsey
                           attribution was once again accepted with a query, but apparently in
                           error. Note the evidence that was provided in MLGB3: "Certainly East
                           Anglian; Ivo, Æthelred, Ælthelbirht, Ægelflæd in the longer litany, and
                           Ægelflæd in the shorter litany suggest Ramsey." This is a direct
                           transcription of the corresponding Medieval Libraries of Great Britain
                           index card for the Ramsey attribution. This card was written by Ker
                           around the 1930s, and it was never updated to reflect the 1987
                           rejection.</p></provenance>
                  </history>
